  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 135 PM CST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nLongwave troughing is expected to push east out of the Intermountain \nWest and across the Rockies Tuesday, finally pushing into the \nnorthern Plains by Wednesday afternoon. This keeps the area under \nthe influence of the southwesterly flow aloft in advance of this \ntrough, along within a persistent low level warm advection regime. \nThis keeps temperatures above average both Tuesday and Wednesday, \nwith highs generally in the 50s to low 60s. By Wednesday afternoon \nas the aforementioned trough begins its trek into the Plains, an \nassociated surface low will eject east out of northeastern Wyoming \nand across the Dakotas. This will bring a cold front through the \narea by late afternoon from west to east. Some light precipitation \nremains possible with the passage of this front, though confidence \nin this remains low at best. Ensemble guidance paints broad \nprobabilities as high as 30-40% of >0.01\" across much of the area, \nthough fall to generally 10% or less for >0.1\". Gusty northwest \nwinds can also be expected Wednesday evening behind the front, with \ngusts up to 30 to 35 miles per hour. \n\nGuidance continues to hint at another system approaching the area by \nlate week and into next weekend, though solutions diverge greatly \nwith respect to both track and strength. Confidence still remains \nlow with respect to both timing and precipitation type, though \nensemble guidance continues to hint at least some measurable \nprecipitation. Recent ensemble solutions continue to paint broad \nprobabilities of >0.1\" as high as 50-70%, with the greatest \nprobabilities north of I-80. Until synoptic details become more \nclear, confidence in exactly what form the precipitation fall in \nwill remain very low. That said, strong northwest winds can be \nexpected on the backside of this system, with probabilities of \n>45mph wind gusts continuing to creep upwards across much of western \nand north central Nebraska. Any impacts remain uncertain for now, \nthough trends will continue to be monitored closely.",
